For encryption, we will use LUKS2, the most recent standard of the Linux
Unified Key Setup specification.

You will be asked for a password. At a later step, we will cover enrolling
hardware tokens, which provide stronger security than most passwords. If you
plan on enrolling a hardware token later on, you can choose a very simple
password at this point as we will wipe its key slot after your token has been
enrolled. Otherwise, you should use a reasonably strong password with sufficient
entropy.
